<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>Azure Key Vault Keys implementation of SharedKernel.Cryptography's IEncryptionKeyProvider and IEnvelopeEncryptionProvider. AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider's direct-retrieval mode (IEncryptionKeyProvider) is built internally on top of its own envelope-wrap mode (IEnvelopeEncryptionProvider, backed by CryptographyClient.WrapKeyAsync/UnwrapKeyAsync) — Azure Key Vault Keys does not export raw HSM-protected key material by default, so the vault's own master key material never crosses the process boundary either way. Fails closed: any Azure SDK exception propagates directly, never a silent fallback. Ships zero caching of its own — compose with SharedKernel.Cryptography's CachedEncryptionKeyProvider externally if bounded-TTL caching is desired. The one 01.Core package with a genuine third-party vendor SDK dependency (Azure.Security.KeyVault.Keys + Azure.Identity), kept out of the zero-third-party-dependency SharedKernel.Cryptography core — mirrors why SharedKernel.Storage.S3/.Obs are separate packages from SharedKernel.Storage.Abstractions. Shipped (P-494/WO-081, additive): a new, deliberately separate class, AzureKeyVaultAsymmetricKeyProvider, implements the async IAsymmetricKeyProvider (P-493) for REMOTE RSA/ECDSA sign/verify — GetRsaKeyAsync/GetEcdsaKeyAsync return a thin RSA/ECDsa subclass whose SignHash/VerifyHash overrides (the real overridable BCL extension points, since SignData/VerifyData are non-virtual) delegate to Azure Key Vault's genuine synchronous CryptographyClient.Sign/Verify calls, mapping the fixed SHA-256+PSS / SHA-256-on-P-256 combinations RsaSignatureService/EcdsaSignatureService already use to Azure's PS256/ES256 algorithms. Zero changes to RsaSignatureService/EcdsaSignatureService were needed. Never exports private key material (ExportParameters/ImportParameters always throw); never implements ISynchronousAsymmetricKeyProvider (every call is a real, unavoidable blocking Key Vault network round trip); caches one CryptographyClient per distinct Azure key name from its first implementation, never per call — the connection-reuse pattern later applied retroactively to AzureKeyVaultEncryptionKeyProvider (P-496).</Description>
    <PackageTags>shared-kernel;cryptography;azure;key-vault;keyvault;kms;envelope-encryption;encryption-key-provider;asymmetric-signing;rsa;ecdsa;remote-signing</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Security.KeyVault.Keys" />
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Identity" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Cryptography\SharedKernel.Cryptography.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Configuration\SharedKernel.Configuration.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
